00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.780
Chef, I'm Julia Child. Whenever you do French onion soup, you just need an awful lot of onions,

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:14.560
and if you're going to have an awful lot of sliced onions, you've got to have a good sharp knife,

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:21.490
and the kind of knife that is used by most professionals for chopping is wedge-shaped,

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:26.630
and it's called a chef's knife, and this one, which I like very much because it's so nice and heavy

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:29.630
that it does most of the work for you, has a blade 11.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.460
inches long but you can use a shorter knife like this which is seven and a

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.880
half inches long but if you have these two sizes 11 and seven and seven and a

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.430
half inches you can slice and chop up just about anything you want and

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:51.670
whenever you have them when you're going to buy knives you have to be sure

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:55.930
that you get a knife that will cut you often hear people say that a knife is

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:59.970
supposed to hold an edge, but if you watch your butcher, and he's all

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.730
sharpening his knife all the time you know that you can't get a knife that

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:08.630
will hold an edge what you want is just a knife that will take an edge a knife

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:12.990
that you can sharpen and I've found that the knives that you can sharpen the

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:19.590
best are plain old-fashioned rustable steel called carbon steel and that's

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.890
what these are these are French ones and I've had them for an awfully long

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:28.410
time but you can now buy them in quite a few of the import shops or as many

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:29.870
hardware and kitchenware.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.340
stores, and if you can't seem to find what you want, ask your butcher, because he'll know

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:40.150
where to get good knives. And again, if you watch your butcher, you'll notice that he's

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:48.760
always sharpening his knife. And to do that, he uses a butcher's steel. And be sure when

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:54.660
you get yourself a butcher's steel, get a great big heavy-duty one. This has a steel that's

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:59.880
12 inches long and it has a guard up here and that is so that when you're holding

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.620
steel and you sharpen the knife you won't come in there and just cut your hand off and then it has

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:11.580
a handle and a thing to hang it up by i got this at my hardware store and it cost just about two

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:22.240
dollars and when you sharpen it you go on each side of the steel and your knife you raise at about a

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:29.910
20 degree angle see just about like that and you come down and up down and up

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.560
always at the 20 degree angle,

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.120
and then you take on the other side,

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:34.440
sharpening the other side of it,

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:37.580
which is down and up, this kind of a movement.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.620
And if your knife is very dull,

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.860
you can go sort of one or two times on each side

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.130
and then finish off like this.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:49.500
And then the knife is so sharp

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.020
that when you feel it, it feels very sharp.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:56.260
And also if you just lay it on top of a tomato,

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.100
just the weight of the knife will cut through the tomato.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:04:59.980
or if you're looking at it,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.810
laid it over your hand and just drew it across.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:05.610
The weight would cut your hand right down to the bone.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:11.970
In other words, a knife is a tool, and it's got to be very sharp.

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:16.090
And they're really, they're your life in your kitchen,

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:20.250
because it makes them, it's so much easier to cook if you have sharp knives.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:24.510
In fact, I find usually when I go visiting and I'm asked to cook,

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:27.910
I can't find any sharp knives, so I always take my own with me.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:29.530
So I conclude that not many.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.180
Householders have sharp knives, but they're so easy to sharpen, and they're not very expensive.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:44.640
But when you do get good knives and keep them nice and sharp, you really have to care for them like a baby.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:51.360
After you finish cutting with them, you should immediately wash them by hand.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:54.260
And the carbon steel one's stained quite easily.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.500
I've got one here that I rub with lemon juice, and it just looks awful.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:59.410
But it's very easy to clean.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:08.100
You just take some steel wool and dampen it just very slightly, wring it out and take some scouring powder

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:15.820
and put it on the side of the sink or in a little jar, and then just rub it over the knife hard.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:24.350
You see, all that stain has come out. That's just lemon juice, but if you let the knife sit around with something like that on it,

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:29.820
it will immediately stain, but as you can see, it cleans off very easily.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:38.880
and then put it under water, but be sure, get into the habit of always having the blunt edge

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:43.100
against your hand. If you have the sharp edge, you could just cut your hand right in two.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:50.810
So get in the habit of that, of using the tool correctly, and then dry it off.

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:58.040
You don't ever want to put a good knife in the dishwasher, and you don't ever want to wash it

00:06:58.040 --> 00:06:59.980
in the sink with a whole lot of other

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.320
instruments because if you do it will other things will bang against the edge

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:10.620
and break it off and for that very same reason you don't want to leave it

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:15.720
lying loose in the drawer use either a knife box or a knife holder or what's

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:21.260
awfully good are these magnetic life hold knife holders like this see it just

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:26.000
sticks on the wall like that and this one's a very strong one this is both

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.860
for tools and knives. They have some rather fancy looking ones.

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:32.670
that aren't as strong as this,

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:35.410
but be sure they've got one that is both for tools and knives,

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:38.250
and then you'll find that your knife will just stay on,

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:43.610
and you'll never have any trouble about hurting the edge.

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:46.370
Then, when you're going to chop onions,

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:48.230
once you've got your sharp knife,

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:52.210
you want to be able to chop things very fast, like this.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:59.780
How fast that goes, and there your onions are all in slices.

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:02.610
And this is something that you have to practice on,

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:04.920
and I'll show you how you start out.

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:09.340
You take your onion, and you cut it through at the root end, like that,

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.160
and then you lay it flat, and you take a few little slices off the root

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.760
and a few little slices off the stem end,

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:21.950
and then laying it flat again, there's your thumb at one end,

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:24.130
and put your fingers at the other end,

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.690
and your fingernails are bent back,

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:29.450
and there are your knuckles sticking out a little bit,

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:29.970
and those...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.220
act as a guide, and your knife you hold with your thumb and your forefinger on the top

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:42.840
of the blade and your fingers gripping around the handle. And you don't put your finger

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:47.240
on top of the blade. You have it under the blade. This may seem a little bit awkward

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:53.080
at first, but the object of this is that your knife should be an extension of your hand.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.140
And if you have your finger on it like that, you don't have quite as much wrist movement

00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:58.440
as you do when you're this way.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:08:59.520
So practice.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:06.320
holding it this way and in about several days it will feel convenient and then when you chop you

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:12.490
come straight down like that and out you'll see now i'm coming straight down and then pushing out a

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:19.960
little bit that's the reason you have this wedge shape you see i'm coming against my knuckle like

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:27.100
that and then i'm with my thumb see i push my fingers back a little bit at each chop that's

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:29.900
That's why you have your thumb, because that guy

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.360
There, I'll do another one.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:33.940
There's the cut side down.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:36.340
Then cut off this little root end,

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:38.970
and then cut off the little stem end,

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:41.860
and then bracing it with your thumb

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:43.920
and your fingers pointed back

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:45.240
and your knife held this way,

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:46.360
come straight down.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.220
You'll have to start doing slowly at first

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:51.400
and then gradually get faster and faster and faster.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:53.540
Like that.

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:58.460
You see if you have really mastered your knife,

00:09:58.540 --> 00:09:59.900
and this will probably take you maybe

00:09:59.900 --> 00:09:59.980
be

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:08.100
a week or maybe two, you can really plow through all two or three pounds of onions in really

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.460
five minutes or less.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:17.760
But these are the kinds of things, if you're going to do any serious cooking, this is the

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:25.610
dog work that you should practice on and get so that you can do it just as fast as possible

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:29.920
because then you won't be upset if you have a recipe.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.020
that says that you have to have eight cups of thinly sliced onions.

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:37.680
It's very easy to do, and you'd rather enjoy it

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:41.940
because it's something that you enjoy, take a pride in.

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:51.780
See now, as soon as the, as soon as the, I'm finished, I dry the knife,

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:56.560
and be sure to dry it also that way so that the sharp end is away from you.

00:10:58.480 --> 00:10:59.900
And now we're going to have, we have.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:03.660
We're going to do onion soup for six to eight people,

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.580
so we want five to six cups of onions,

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:09.740
or that would be one and a half to two pounds

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:11.460
of yellow onions.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:14.490
And you notice that I was chopping those onions

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:16.210
and I didn't cry at all.

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:18.330
And I think that's one of the main reasons

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:20.510
is because I've got this great sharp knife,

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:22.570
and it cuts right through the onions,

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.210
and so they don't splatter.

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:25.630
If I had a dull knife,

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:29.470
the onion juice would splatter up into my eyes.

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:31.800
And now after you've cut all the onions,

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:35.940
you can get the onion's taste off your hands very easily.

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:41.920
Wash them first in cold water, rinse them off,

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:45.460
and then sprinkle about a teaspoon of salt

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:48.860
in your wet hands, and then rub the salt in,

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:53.170
and wash it off, and then wash it again in warm water.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:57.060
And if you find that you still have a little bit

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:59.600
of onion on your hands, just repeat the process.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.680
You'll find that this will get all the onion off very easily.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:08.780
And now, when you've got all of your onions chopped up,

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:11.200
you're going to then cook them.

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:15.510
And this soup calls for browned onions.

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:21.740
And we have five to six cups for six people.

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:26.820
And we're going to cook them in oil and butter.

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:29.710
And I'm going to...

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:33.780
Put in about a tablespoon of cooking oil

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:37.380
and about two tablespoons of butter.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:44.170
And then in go the onions.

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:53.780
And now this, the, I think you'll find

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:56.380
with this onion soup, if you're gonna make it all yourself,

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:59.360
you're gonna get much the best flavor if you use.

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:05.170
fresh onions because you want to get i mean it's onion soup so you've got to get the most flavor

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:13.710
that you can and then you want to stir the onions around until they're fully covered with your oil

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:19.120
and butter and then to brown onions you you do it in two steps the first one is called in french

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:25.980
etive which means cooking the onions until they're tender and then after you've cooked them until

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:27.440
until they're tender, then you brown them.

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:29.940
But to do this etuve, or cooking...

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:37.660
until tender, you cook them over moderate heat with a cover on for about 20 minutes

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:39.340
until they're tender and translucent.

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:45.280
Because if you tried to brown the onions right now, you'd have browned, sort of raw onions

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:46.740
and they wouldn't taste very good.

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:53.740
So when you're doing the onions, this part takes somewhat of a long time, but it's this,

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:59.960
if you're making your own onion soup, which is so very important, is to brown them properly.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:11.330
So cook them covered for about 20 minutes, and then when they're done, they've cooked down, and see the difference.

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:18.210
Those five or six cups now have cooked down to about, say, about a cup and a half.

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:29.810
And they're nice and tender, and you could, at this point, you could use the onions to make a white onion soup if you wanted to.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:37.480
and then when they've cooked down this way you are ready to brown them and for that you set them

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:46.140
over moderately high heat and you're going to it's going to take you about 20 to 30 minutes to brown

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:52.400
them properly and to help them brown you add a little pinch of sugar and you also add some salt

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:58.680
and i left the salt way over here while i was washing my hands you don't want very much sugar but

00:14:58.680 --> 00:14:59.960
Just this little touch.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.720
a tiny bit, about, say, half to a quarter of a teaspoonful.

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:08.620
The sugar will caramelize, and that helps the onions to brown.

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:12.520
And then we'll put in about a teaspoon of salt.

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:22.720
And then you have to keep stirring them fairly frequently,

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:26.340
so, in other words, when you're cooking them, stay quite near them.

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:29.540
And you'll notice, as they start browning, they will begin...

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.500
attaching a little bit to the bottom of the pan.

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:35.840
Be sure that you have a heavy bottom pan.

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:38.220
Otherwise, if you have a thin bottom pan,

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:41.560
you'll find that the onions will burn on the bottom.

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:44.490
Then you just keep stirring them around.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:49.520
And finally, they will brown and look like this.

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:51.070
And this is amazing.

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:57.460
I'll take these things out so you can see.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:15:59.980
But look at how little there are.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.880
of those onions that you started out with with five or six cups because

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:11.160
there's so much water in them and so when you have your onions brown like this

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:15.790
you can use them for all kinds of other things saying smothering steaks or

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:21.080
hamburgers in onions and these are just perfectly delicious just the way they

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:27.400
are and now we're ready to start making a soup out of them and we want first to

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:29.760
You have about three tablespoons.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:37.350
of flour in because this gives the soup a little body and thickening. So that's one heaping

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:48.900
tablespoon and one level tablespoon and that makes three tablespoons. And this you want to cook over

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:54.260
sort of moderate heat and if you find that you don't have quite enough butter in just add a

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:58.060
little bit more because you want to make a paste out of it because you want to cook the flour

00:16:58.060 --> 00:16:59.660
and brown it lightly.

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:12.120
And this also, you don't want to, you want to do rather slowly so that you brown it nicely.

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:17.020
I think I have my heat a little bit too high there now.

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:24.390
And this, you would cook about two or three minutes.

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:29.690
I'm not going to do the whole thing, but when you do it, you cook it for two or three minutes until the flour slowly.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:37.140
browns and then you're ready to add your add the liquid to it and if you're going to do onion soup

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:44.240
yourself it would be too bad to use canned stock with it because it has beef stock if you had canned

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:48.540
stock then it would taste just like canned onion soup so you might just as well have started out

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:53.740
with canned onion soup in the first place and so you'd have a homemade stock and this is a great

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:59.600
mixture of beef shanks and chicken necks and water and vegetables.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:07.350
and this is simmered for five or six hours and it looks awful but it's perfectly delicious and i want

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:16.700
to have about six cups in all but i'm just going to put in a little bit first and then stir it

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:22.860
around to make sure that it's blended nicely with the flour and also so that i've scraped up all the

00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:29.970
brown sort of brown onion juices in the bottom of the pan as a matter of

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.970
In fact, I think I should stir that up with a wire whip

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:36.230
to make sure that the flour's all mixed up nicely.

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:46.380
And then the rest of the stock goes in about six cups.

00:18:48.020 --> 00:18:49.620
If you're gonna make a stock yourself,

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:51.260
we've done a lot of them,

00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:56.240
particularly I remember a great turkey stock.

00:18:56.380 --> 00:18:59.500
Just save all the bones and things and then buy.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:01.860
a little bit of fresh meat, and all you need to do

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:06.220
is just to put water and carrots and onions

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:09.080
and celery and salt in, and then just simmer it

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:12.490
when you come up with a perfectly beautiful stock.

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:16.620
And then set this over heat and let it bring up

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:21.500
to the simmer, and then this, of course,

00:19:21.580 --> 00:19:24.840
is now only browned onions and beef stock,

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:27.680
but you want to add some little French touches,

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:29.900
such as wine and herbs.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.660
And you can use either white wine or red wine.

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:35.900
I'm going to use some California mountain red wine.

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:38.920
And put in just about a cup there.

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:42.120
I don't know if that is quite a cup.

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:43.060
I'll put in a little bit more.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:44.300
But it's all going to cook down.

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:48.290
And then put in some herbs like a bay leaf.

00:19:48.790 --> 00:19:54.810
And I'm going to use about a half a teaspoon of powdered sage.

00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:58.840
And then that is to cook very slowly.

00:19:59.160 --> 00:19:59.960
You don't even need to cook.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.760
coverage, you can partially cook it, but let it cook very slowly for 30 or 40

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:09.380
minutes to get all the flavor out. And now if you were using canned or

00:20:09.380 --> 00:20:13.060
dehydrated onion soups, and there's some awfully good ones on the market, just

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:17.740
follow the package directions, and then when you've rehydrated it or heated it

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:24.120
up, then to add your own touches to it, then put in some wine and bay leaf and

00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:28.880
herbs and let that simmer for 20 or 30 minutes, because you have to when

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:29.940
when you're using one.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:39.590
you have to let it cook out and then let it simmer until until it's until it tastes perfectly

00:20:39.590 --> 00:20:46.810
delicious because it's cooked down and that's all there is to the cooking and be sure then before

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:52.470
you're ready to serve it that you've very very carefully tasted it has just the right amount of

00:20:52.470 --> 00:20:57.790
salt and pepper and everything else and you can do it way ahead of time and just let it sit if you

00:20:57.790 --> 00:20:59.970
want and you could

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:05.380
serve it perfectly plain, just as it is. Just serve it in soup cups with some French bread

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:11.640
and cheese. Or if you want to be very French, you will serve croutes with it. And croutes

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:19.160
are spelled C for crust. R-O-U-T-E-S. And that's just what they are. They're little crusts

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:26.550
of toasted French bread, which are sort of nice and crisp, sort of hard toasted French

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:28.430
and they're perfectly delicious.

00:21:28.790 --> 00:21:29.970
And I think...

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:33.060
they add a great deal to French onion soup.

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:36.980
Now we're going to slice,

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:41.750
this takes some homemade type white bread,

00:21:41.930 --> 00:21:43.810
French bread if possible,

00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:47.060
and cut it into one inch slices.

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:49.820
I'll just make four of these.

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:57.140
Then place it on a baking sheet.

00:22:02.490 --> 00:22:07.190
And then you bake it in a 325 oven for 15 minutes on each side.

00:22:07.190 --> 00:22:10.890
And what I like to do is to take a little light olive oil

00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:16.410
and paint each side of them with a little oil.

00:22:16.630 --> 00:22:19.480
And that just seems to give them a little better flavor.

00:22:19.720 --> 00:22:27.870
And that's just half an hour in a 325 oven, and they're all made.

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:29.500
And they come out.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:35.210
like that and I always make quite a quite a few extra ones because they're just

00:22:35.210 --> 00:22:41.520
because they're so good but what we're going to do also with these croupes is

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:49.830
to make onion soup gratiné and you can you this is going to be baked in the

00:22:49.830 --> 00:22:54.330
oven you can use any kind of a casserole baking dish souffle dish whatever you

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:59.350
like. This is a French earthenware casserole which

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:07.660
is sort of typical so i'm going to use that now this we will say that this soup is simmered for

00:23:07.660 --> 00:23:14.500
30 or 40 minutes and is perfect in every respect and so you you could serve it in this casserole

00:23:14.500 --> 00:23:21.880
i have but i'm just going to be fancy and put it in the earthenware casserole there goes all the

00:23:21.880 --> 00:23:28.610
onion in then you can add some more French touches such as a little bit of

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:29.870
cognac

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:36.200
about two or three tablespoons that certainly wouldn't ever hurt any soup at all and that all

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:42.630
cooks down and then you can add some more and this is particularly useful if you're doing

00:23:42.630 --> 00:23:50.330
using bottom soup take a raw onion and grate that and you think you've had enough onions in but this

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:59.900
little grated raw onion adds a that je ne sais quoi and then so it'll be cheesy and sticky

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:03.980
you know, so that when you lift it up, it springs out.

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:07.950
Take some Swiss cheese and just slice it.

00:24:08.090 --> 00:24:11.440
Well, this is probably about, I'll use about two ounces,

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:15.260
and slice it through with the grater, and that goes in.

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:23.040
And that's gonna give it that sort of nice stringy quality.

00:24:24.250 --> 00:24:28.070
And then just take your toasted croupes,

00:24:28.150 --> 00:24:29.090
and they go on top.

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:42.560
and then the cheese goes on top and this you would use a combination of grated swiss cheese

00:24:43.100 --> 00:24:47.810
and parmesan cheese and be sure that you put a big thick layer on like about

00:24:47.810 --> 00:24:52.330
half an inch and be sure that you get all the toast covered because when it goes into the oven

00:24:52.330 --> 00:24:59.310
if you don't have all the toast covered it's going to some of the toast will burn and then take a

00:24:59.310 --> 00:24:59.850
little bit of-

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:10.630
olive oil or melted butter and pour that over the cheese and that helps it brown then this should

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:16.090
be done just at the last minute and then you're ready to put it into the oven and this bakes in

00:25:16.090 --> 00:25:27.410
a 350 degree oven and this bakes for just about half an hour and i have one here that's been baked

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:29.530
for just about half an hour.

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:41.050
out she comes you see that's all bubbling nicely but the cheese hasn't browned very much so that

00:25:41.050 --> 00:25:48.620
so then you put it under the broiler for just about a minute until the just to brown the

00:25:48.620 --> 00:25:59.800
cheese very lightly you guys it's so hot i better use some pot holders i tried thinking

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:07.880
that I could both brown the cheese at the same time as baking it in the oven the other

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:15.400
day and I put one in a 425 degree oven and found that it bubbled up so much that all

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:21.040
the croutons sort of got, had a tumbled effect, so that didn't work at all. So you have to

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:29.570
do it in two stages. Now while this is browning, here if you can of course serve the soup

00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:29.970
A little if.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:37.480
individual cups or and you don't have to gratine it. So you just put a crout in the bottom and then

00:26:37.480 --> 00:26:47.260
pour some onion soup on the top and then just put on some cheese and you can

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:52.950
serve it like that or you or if you if you want to make more of a meal of it

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:59.200
You can put the crout on and then you can put a poached egg on top of the crout and then the cheese on top of that

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:07.180
The way this is now, you could put, say, two or three croupes in and just bake this, bake and brown this in the oven.

00:27:07.220 --> 00:27:10.260
So you have all kinds of ways to serve it.

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:12.260
Now I better go back before that.

00:27:14.360 --> 00:27:16.550
It's really browning here.

00:27:16.770 --> 00:27:17.890
Oh, that's beautiful.

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:20.810
There you are.

00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:26.740
I think that possibly browned a little bit too much, but I don't know.

00:27:26.820 --> 00:27:28.640
It gives a very good effect.

00:27:30.320 --> 00:27:32.500
Remember, when you're going to do the gratinay,

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:36.670
you can get the soup done as far ahead as you want.

00:27:36.770 --> 00:27:38.770
As a matter of fact, I think if you did it a day ahead,

00:27:38.790 --> 00:27:40.750
it would probably even taste better.

00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:44.250
But don't do the gratinay until the very last minute

00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:46.210
and then take it right into the dining room

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:54.130
because you want it to be bubbling when it comes in.

00:27:56.570 --> 00:27:58.150
Then you're ready to serve it.

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:02.300
You have a big ladle.

00:28:04.650 --> 00:28:08.630
You usually have to hop down through the top a little bit.

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:11.430
And there you are.

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:21.980
And that's a wonderful smell and a very appetizing one.

00:28:21.980 --> 00:28:25.660
And if you want to serve wine, I think something like a

00:28:25.660 --> 00:28:29.980
California Mountain Red or a Beaujolais go extremely well with it.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:31.800
And this is such a gratiné soup.

00:28:32.720 --> 00:28:36.050
It's very rich with all that cheese and toast in it.

00:28:36.050 --> 00:28:40.310
And I really think you don't need much else along with your meal than the green salad.

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:43.430
And you might want to follow it with fruit.

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:48.070
So this is really the soup that made the onion famous.

00:28:48.190 --> 00:28:54.710
And it's your own French onion soup, whether you started it from scratch or from out of the can.

00:28:54.910 --> 00:28:59.960
When you've added all these little French touches, who's going to know?

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:07.560
So, that's all for today on The French Chef. This is Julia Child. Bon appétit!

